{
  "dom_id": "step-q79232",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,3,2)$ where $t=-1$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-1)=k,\\,N(-1)=i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,3,2)$ is $p=i+3\\,j+2\\,k.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-1)$ and the principal normal $N(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(-1)\\times N(-1) \\\\  & =k\\times i \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|(i+3\\,j+2\\,k)\\cdot j| \\\\  & =|0+3+0| \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
